# Netlify serves everything with "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" by
# default, which means the browser re-checks EVERY image and audio file with
# the server on EVERY view. For the extension that is a network round-trip
# per hover, and it is why the extension degrades the moment this site is
# slow or unreachable: nothing is ever really cached.
#
# Card illustrations and audio are generated once and addressed by concept id
# or content hash, so they are safe to cache hard. 30 days rather than a year
# with `immutable` is deliberate: some audio files HAVE been corrected in
# place before (the katakana "to " fix), and `immutable` would leave those
# users stuck with the old file until they cleared their cache. 30 days keeps
# essentially all of the benefit with a bounded worst case.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# Data files change on deploy and the service worker already fetches them
# network-first, so they must stay revalidated.
#
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * lets the browser extension fetch() these
# same public, unauthenticated data files (JSON, no user data, already
# served with no auth to anyone) directly from picturethewords.com rather
# than bundling a copy locally -- confirmed missing via a live curl check
# (2026-09-20) before assuming it was already there: without this header a
# cross-origin fetch() from a chrome-extension:// origin is blocked by the
# browser even though the same URL loads fine for a same-origin <img>/
# <audio> tag, which needs no CORS grant at all. This is what makes the
# extension's sentence search (reading sentence-search-index.json) and any
# other future extension feature that needs to *read* a /data/* file,
# rather than just point a media tag at it, possible without shipping a
# multi-megabyte copy inside the extension package.
/data/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
